# THREE PARTS, PUT TOGETHER (M23).
#
# The M23 gate: three parts go into an assembly, get moved to where they
# belong, and the whole thing is saved and can be opened again with everything
# still where it was put.
#
# Run it:
#   ep3d --script examples/assembly-three-parts.ep3ds
#
# One script does both halves -- it BUILDS the three parts, saves them, and
# then assembles them. That is not a convenience: an assembly stores the PATH
# to each part, so the parts have to be real files before anything can be
# inserted, and a script that could only do one half could not show that.

# --- part 1: the base plate ---------------------------------------------------

sketch Plate
tool rect
click -60 -40
click 60 40
pad Base 10

echo --- the base plate ---
solve
measure
save build/asm-base.ep3d

# --- part 2: a post -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# A new script would normally start a new document. This one keeps going, so
# the second part is a second BODY -- and `save` writes the whole document, so
# the file holds both. That is why the inserts below name a body: a file with
# two bodies in it and no name given is REFUSED, with the names it does have,
# rather than resolved to whichever came first.

sketch Column
tool circle
click 0 0
click 12 0
pad Post 50

echo --- the post ---
solve
measure
save build/asm-parts.ep3d

# --- the assembly -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# From here on `solve`, `measure` and `save` are about the ASSEMBLY. The part
# verbs still work; what changed is what those three words are about, and the
# log says so when the switch happens.

assembly Rig

# An instance names a FILE and a BODY inside it -- the sentence "that body, in
# that file". Not a copy of the geometry: the part file is the source of
# truth, so a part that is edited shows up here on the next rebuild, and a part
# whose file goes away stops its instance BY NAME rather than leaving a copy
# nobody can trace back to anything.
insert Plate build/asm-base.ep3d Base

# The same part file, twice, at two places. Three instances of one part is the
# case an assembly exists for, and the case any cache of "the part" would
# break.
insert PostLeft build/asm-parts.ep3d Post
insert PostRight build/asm-parts.ep3d Post

# WHERE. The placement is a reference frame -- there is no second transform
# anywhere -- so it is undoable, it dirties exactly what it should through an
# ordinary graph edge, and a sub-assembly in M26 is a frame parented to a frame
# rather than a new way of saying where something is.
place Plate 0 0 0
place PostLeft -40 0 10
place PostRight 40 0 10 45

# The plate is 120 x 80 x 10 = 96000, and each post is pi * 12^2 * 50 = 22619.5,
# so the three together are about 141239. The CENTRES are what carry the claim:
# a volume alone would be identical for three parts all sitting on top of each
# other at the origin.
echo --- three parts, each somewhere ---
solve
measure

save build/rig.ep3da
